466 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Economies of scale can be internal or external. Internal economies result in a reduction in the long run average costs of a firm as the scale of production increases. There are several sources of internal economies of scale. These include technological, management, marketing, finance and procurement. External economies of scale benefit all firms as the scale of the whole industry increases. These include; a pool of skilled labour, shared research facilities and shared infrastructure. Economies of scale will encourage firms to grow as they will benefit from the reduction in long run average costs. This reduction might allow them to reduce the price of their product\(s\) and thus gain a competitive edge in the market. They may decide to keep price the same but earn more abnormal profit. In turn this will allow greater investment in the firm. The firm will become more internationally competitive and have access to a larger market. The extent to which economies of scale exist vary in different industries. Firms in some industries may be able to take advantage of economies of scale over a large range of outputs than in other industries. Other factors influencing growth might include the level of competition determined by the existence or lack of barriers to entry or the number of competitors and the behaviour of those competitors in the market. Other factors influencing growth will be the level of differentiation of the product and)
/E (Economies of scale can be internal or external. Internal economies result in a reduction in the long run average costs of a firm as the scale of production increases. There are several sources of internal economies of scale. These include technological, management, marketing, finance and procurement. External economies of scale benefit all firms as the scale of the whole industry increases. These include; a pool of skilled labour, shared research facilities and shared infrastructure. Economies of scale will encourage firms to grow as they will benefit from the reduction in long run average costs. This reduction might allow them to reduce the price of their product\(s\) and thus gain a competitive edge in the market. They may decide to keep price the same but earn more abnormal profit. In turn this will allow greater investment in the firm. The firm will become more internationally competitive and have access to a larger market. The extent to which economies of scale exist vary in different industries. Firms in some industries may be able to take advantage of economies of scale over a large range of outputs than in other industries. Other factors influencing growth might include the level of competition determined by the existence or lack of barriers to entry or the number of competitors and the behaviour of those competitors in the market. Other factors influencing growth will be the level of differentiation of the product and)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
