<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (This is particularly problematic in the case of public utilities because they are necessity goods. Firms will enjoy significant supernormal profits that will benefit shareholders but not service users, who experience little consumer surplus as prices are high. Firms have no incentive to undertake investment because they do not face competition. Nationalising these firms would mean supernormal profits are redistributed to service users in the form of lower prices and/or greater investment in the quality of the service.)
/E (This is particularly problematic in the case of public utilities because they are necessity goods. Firms will enjoy significant supernormal profits that will benefit shareholders but not service users, who experience little consumer surplus as prices are high. Firms have no incentive to undertake investment because they do not face competition. Nationalising these firms would mean supernormal profits are redistributed to service users in the form of lower prices and/or greater investment in the quality of the service.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [6]
>>
